{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And as for me, when I came from Padan, etc.</b> This verse has <b>no</b> connection with what precedes it. Nor is the intent of this statement known. And what the commentators of blessed memory (Rashi, Lekach Tov) explained, that his intent is, ״Even though I troubled you to carry me for burial in the land of Canaan, I did not do so״ and so forth — up to here. According to their words, this is not the place for these words but rather above (Genesis 47:30), when he said to him, ״and you shall carry me out of Egypt and bury me״, etc.; it was adjacent to that that he should have said ״And as for me, when I came״, etc. Further, what is his intent in saying the word <b>upon me</b>?" "<b>However</b>, according to what I explained — that he informs him that the sons assured through him that they would issue from him are these — he intended in saying <b>And as for me, when I came</b>, etc., that he is the cause that they did not issue from him literally, in that he caused Rachel's death, and that is his saying <b>she died upon me</b> — meaning, on my account — as they said of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah, chapter 37), that he delayed his vow; or because of the curse he uttered, as he said, ״With whomever you find your gods, he shall not live״, and had Rachel lived they would have issued from him, and now they issue from Joseph, who is an aspect of Jacob and also an aspect of Rachel." "It is possible that Jacob intended to appease Joseph for having caused his mother's death before her time, and he said that the reason he reckoned his two sons as Reuben and Simon was because Rachel died upon him and he brought on the proximity of her death. And the reason he prolonged further to say <b>while there was still some distance of land</b>, etc., he intended to say what the commentators of blessed memory expounded — that he intended to say, even though he did not do so for his mother, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {0 "The phrase מֵתָה עָלַי / עָלַי (\"upon me\") is the word under discussion in the final question; the bolding reflects the Hebrew lemma marking in the source.", 1 "The verse citation בראשית מ״ז:ל׳ appears in comment [0]; here Leviticus Rabbah chapter 37 is cited. The phrase אָכֵן ('however/truly') is the lemma opening this comment, marked bold in the source as a discourse marker."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:03.081339083Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}